<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Niagara Escarpment</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Niagara Escarpment is a long ridge of resistant dolomitic limestone running from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> State through southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a> and around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wisconsin">Wisconsin</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niagara-river">Niagara River</a> plunges over it at Niagara Falls. Its outcrops provided chert for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">stone tools</a> of indigenous peoples such as the Neutral, and the Ontario section is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> biosphere reserve.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/niagara-escarpment.jpg" alt="Niagara Escarpment" width="320" height="184" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Niagara_Escarpment_map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The escarpment is an arc-shaped, broken ridge about 1,050 kilometres long, generally facing north, that runs round the top of the Great Lakes Basin. In Rochester, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genesee-river">Genesee River</a> falls over it in three waterfalls; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a> it follows the Lake Ontario shore through St. Catharines and Hamilton, turns north at Milton towards Georgian Bay, and forms the spine of the Bruce Peninsula and Manitoulin Island, before crossing the Upper Peninsula of Michigan and the Door Peninsula to end in Dodge County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wisconsin">Wisconsin</a>.</p><p>It is not a fault line but the product of unequal erosion. A cap of hard dolostone of the Silurian Lockport Formation, laid down on the floor of an Ordovician-Silurian tropical sea, overlies softer shale; as streams wear the shale away, the caprock is undercut and a cliff is left. The process is quickest at Niagara Falls, and in places, as north of Georgetown, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a>, glacial deposits such as the Oak Ridges Moraine bury the ridge.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In High Cliff State Park in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wisconsin">Wisconsin</a>, animal and geometric effigy mounds of the escarpment's early inhabitants stand beside the remains of a limestone quarry and kiln of the early 20th century. The ridge was a major obstacle to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erie-canal">Erie Canal</a>, which climbed it by a series of locks, and the town that grew there took the name Lockport, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. The Welland Canal later carried ships across it between Lake Erie and Lake Ontario. Hamilton, Ontario, straddles the ridge, which residents call the Mountain, and from 1892 to 1936 the Hamilton Incline Railway carried people up and down it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> designated the escarpment a World Biosphere Reserve in February 1990, and the Niagara Escarpment Commission, an agency of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a> government, regulates land use along it. Its cliffs hold the oldest forest ecosystem in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, including an eastern white cedar dating from 688, the oldest tree in Ontario. The Bruce Trail follows it from Queenston to Tobermory, and wine regions lie along it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, Ontario and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wisconsin">Wisconsin</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Niagara Escarpment” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They grew maize and tobacco, quarried chert on the <b>Niagara Escarpment</b>, and traded with both sides.</blockquote><a href="/state/neutral-confederacy">Neutral Confederacy</a></li></ul></section>
